The situation CI faces is a COMMITTED manifest that no longer matches the
lockfiles. The three candidate gate orders are therefore measured on a
tamper that is COMMITTED, not merely left in the working tree: a
tamper that was never committed is simply overwritten by the next
regeneration and says nothing about what the gate can detect.

This section runs in a throwaway clone, so the tampered commit never
reaches the deliverable.

-------------------------------------------------------------------
order A: regenerate, then --check
-------------------------------------------------------------------
(the order this gate used to have: it compares the file it just wrote
 with itself, so any committed drift is silently overwritten)

tampered: "integrity": "sha512-Xz4Tpyki7XyrpbUK1jR1
26953be probe: tampered manifest
exit code = 0   (0 means the drift was NOT detected)

-------------------------------------------------------------------
order B: --check first
-------------------------------------------------------------------
(the order this gate has now: it compares the committed file against
 the lockfiles, without touching the file)

exit code = 1   (non-zero means the drift WAS detected)

-------------------------------------------------------------------
order C: regenerate, then git diff --exit-code
-------------------------------------------------------------------
(measured on its own, starting again from a tampered commit)

exit code = 1   (non-zero means the drift WAS detected)

So the detection capability of the three orders is:
  order A (regenerate, then --check) : does NOT detect
  order B (--check first)            : detects
  order C (regenerate, then git diff): detects

Order C detects the drift because the regeneration REWRITES the
committed manifest, so the working tree differs from the commit.
Order B is the one this gate uses: it is the only order that does not
write to the file it is checking, needs no git, and runs offline.

